\section{Dual–Term Objective for Prompt–Pooled Continual Learning}

In the prompt‑pool framework the learner optimises three groups of parameters simultaneously:

\begin{itemize}
    \item $P=\{p_{1},\dots ,p_{|P|}\}$ – the \emph{prompt pool}, i.e. the set of learnable prompt vectors that are stored as values in a key–value memory.
    \item $K=\{k_{1},\dots ,k_{|K|}\}$ – the \emph{key bank} associated with the prompts.  Each key $k_{s_i}$ lives in the same latent space as a query vector $q(x)$ and is used to retrieve the most relevant prompt(s) for an input $x$.
    \item $\phi$ – the parameters of the classifier head $g_{\phi}(\cdot)$ that maps the (prompt‑augmented) token representation to class logits.
\end{itemize}

The backbone visual encoder, a Vision Transformer (ViT) denoted $f_{r}^{\text{avg}}(\cdot)$, is kept frozen throughout training.  Freezing the backbone preserves the generic visual features that have been pre‑trained on a large corpus, while learning $P$, $K$ and $\phi$ enables the system to adapt to a stream of tasks without incurring catastrophic forgetting.

\subsection{Formal Statement of the Loss}

The overall objective is a \emph{dual‑term loss}:

\begin{equation}
\label{eq:dual_term}
\min_{P,K,\phi}\;
\underbrace{\mathcal{L}\!\bigl(g_{\phi}\bigl(f_{r}^{\text{avg}}(x_{p})\bigr),\,y\bigr)}_{\text{(i) classification loss}}
\;+\;
\lambda \underbrace{\sum_{k_{s_i}\in K_{x}} \gamma\!\bigl(q(x),\,k_{s_i}\bigr)}_{\text{(ii) query–key alignment}}
\end{equation}

where:

\begin{description}
    \item[$x_{p}$] the input image $x$ after it has been \emph{prompt‑augmented}; i.e. the selected prompt(s) from $P$ have been concatenated to the token sequence fed to the frozen ViT.
    \item[$y$] the ground‑truth class label for $x$.
    \item[$\mathcal{L}(\cdot,\cdot)$] the standard softmax cross‑entropy loss:
    \[
        \mathcal{L}\bigl(\hat{y},y\bigr)= -\log\frac{\exp(\hat{y}_{y})}{\sum_{c}\exp(\hat{y}_{c})},
    \]
    with $\hat{y}=g_{\phi}\!\bigl(f_{r}^{\text{avg}}(x_{p})\bigr)$ the predicted logit vector.
    \item[$q(x)$] the \emph{query vector} produced by a lightweight function (often a linear projection) applied to the frozen representation $f_{r}^{\text{avg}}(x)$.  $q(x)$ lives in the same space as the keys.
    \item[$K_{x}$] the subset of keys retrieved for $x$ (e.g. the top‑$M$ nearest neighbours of $q(x)$ under a similarity metric).
    \item[$\gamma(\cdot,\cdot)$] a similarity (or distance) function that measures alignment between query and key.  Typical choices are the negative cosine distance,
    \[
        \gamma\bigl(q,k\bigr)= -\,\frac{q^{\top}k}{\|q\|\;\|k\|},
    \]
    or a squared Euclidean distance.
    \item[$\lambda$] a scalar hyper‑parameter controlling the trade‑off between accurate classification (term (i)) and faithful retrieval of relevant prompts (term (ii)).
\end{description}

\subsection{Interpretation of the Two Terms}

\paragraph{(i) Classification loss.}
Term (i) drives the classifier head $\phi$ to produce correct predictions from the feature map that already incorporates the selected prompts.  Because the backbone is frozen, all task‑specific adaptation must be realised through $P$ and $\phi$.

\paragraph{(ii) Surrogate query–key loss.}
Term (ii) is a \emph{surrogate} that encourages the key vectors to be close to the queries they are intended to serve.  When the distance $\gamma\bigl(q(x),k_{s_i}\bigr)$ is minimised, the key $k_{s_i}$ will be retrieved more often for inputs whose queries are similar, thereby pulling the associated prompt $p_{s_i}$ into the forward pass for those inputs.  This mechanism tightens the coupling between the external memory (prompt pool) and the instance‑wise retrieval process described in the previous section.

\subsection{Optimization Procedure}

All learnable parameters $\{P,K,\phi\}$ are updated with the Adam optimiser \cite{kingma2014adam}.  For each mini‑batch $\{(x^{(b)},y^{(b)})\}_{b=1}^{B}$ the following steps are performed:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Compute the frozen representation $z^{(b)} = f_{r}^{\text{avg}}(x^{(b)})$.
    \item Obtain the query $q^{(b)} = \mathcal{Q}(z^{(b)})$, where $\mathcal{Q}$ is the linear projection that defines $q(\cdot)$.
    \item Retrieve the top‑$M$ keys $K_{x^{(b)}}$ nearest to $q^{(b)}$ under the similarity measure used in $\gamma$.
    \item Assemble the prompt‑augmented token sequence $x^{(b)}_{p}$ by concatenating the prompts $\{p_{s_i}\}$ whose keys belong to $K_{x^{(b)}}$.
    \item Forward $x^{(b)}_{p}$ through the frozen ViT, obtain logits $\hat{y}^{(b)} = g_{\phi}\bigl(f_{r}^{\text{avg}}(x^{(b)}_{p})\bigr)$, and compute the cross‑entropy loss.
    \item Compute the surrogate loss $\sum_{k_{s_i}\in K_{x^{(b)}}} \gamma\bigl(q^{(b)},k_{s_i}\bigr)$.
    \item Form the total loss according to Eq.~\eqref{eq:dual_term}, back‑propagate gradients \emph{only} through $P$, $K$, and $\phi$, and update them with Adam.
\end{enumerate}

Because gradients are blocked from flowing into the frozen ViT, the optimisation focuses exclusively on shaping the external memory (keys and prompts) and the classifier.  This design aligns with the continual‑learning objective: the backbone provides a stable feature extractor, while the mutable prompt pool supplies the plasticity needed to accommodate new tasks without overwriting previously acquired knowledge.

\subsection{Role of the Trade‑off Parameter $\lambda$}

The hyper‑parameter $\lambda$ balances two possibly competing desiderata:

\begin{itemize}
    \item A large $\lambda$ forces the keys to align tightly with queries, leading to very precise retrieval of prompts.  However, if $\lambda$ dominates, the model may over‑fit the memory structure at the expense of classification accuracy.
    \item A small $\lambda$ reduces the influence of the surrogate term, allowing the classifier head to rely more on the frozen ViT features.  This can degrade the system’s ability to exploit task‑specific prompts, especially when the new task diverges from the pre‑training distribution.
\end{itemize}

In practice $\lambda$ is tuned on a held‑out validation stream that mimics the continual‑learning setting (e.g., a sequence of tasks with limited rehearsal).

\subsection{Connection to Instance‑Wise Prompt Querying}

Recall that the prompt pool is conceptualised as a key–value store: a query $q(x)$ selects the most relevant \emph{value} (prompt) via its associated key.  The surrogate loss in Eq.~\eqref{eq:dual_term} directly operationalises this concept by penalising mismatches between queries and their retrieved keys.  Consequently, as training proceeds the geometry of the key space reshapes so that semantically similar inputs (according to the frozen ViT representation) map to nearby keys, which in turn retrieve the same or similar prompts.  This self‑organising behaviour is the mechanism that mitigates catastrophic forgetting: prompts that have been useful for earlier tasks remain reachable for future inputs that share the same key neighbourhood, while new prompts can be added without altering the frozen backbone.

\subsection{Summary}

The dual‑term loss in Equation~\eqref{eq:dual_term} provides a principled training objective for a continual‑learning system that leverages a frozen Vision Transformer and an external prompt pool.  The first term guarantees discriminative performance on the current task, whereas the second term enforces a tight coupling between query vectors and memory keys, ensuring that the instance‑wise retrieval mechanism remains effective throughout the lifelong learning trajectory.  Optimisation with Adam only updates the mutable components ($P$, $K$, $\phi$), preserving the stability of the pretrained visual encoder while granting the flexibility needed to acquire new knowledge incrementally.

